<div class="page-body two-col"><div><section class="now-entry"><p class="eyebrow blue">01 / Building</p><h2>A more predictable platform.</h2><p>I’m putting together a GitOps reference architecture for multi-tenant, multi-cluster EKS footprints. The focus is a delivery path that stays understandable when more than a dozen product squads share the platform.</p><a class="text-link" href="/work/#multi-cloud">The work this builds on ↗</a></section><section class="now-entry"><p class="eyebrow blue">02 / Exploring</p><h2>Context, agents, and boundaries.</h2><p>I’ve been exploring MCP servers and context providers that bring operational information into developer workspaces. I’m interested in how agents reason about infrastructure-as-code and configuration drift—and what permissions that requires.</p><a class="text-link" href="/work/#agents">My agent-boundary exploration ↗</a></section><section class="now-entry"><p class="eyebrow blue">03 / Reading</p><h2>Ideas I want to spend time with.</h2><p>My 2026 reading plan mixes technical leadership, systems, and decision-making. I’m revisiting consistency tradeoffs in <em>Designing Data-Intensive Applications</em> and making space for <em>The Staff Engineer’s Path</em>.</p><a class="text-link" href="/blog/2026-01-06-books-to-read-in-2026/">The full reading list ↗</a></section></div><aside class="side-note"><p class="eyebrow blue">A question I’m carrying</p><h2>Where does an agent’s authority stop?</h2><p>It’s easy to draw an arrow from an agent to a tool. I’m more interested in what has to be true before the arrow is allowed to exist.</p><a href="/blog/2026-03-28-why-ai-agents-will-break-your-identity-platform/">Read the note ↗</a></aside></div>
